  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.5 z²) / 2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. It is steeper along x, so overshooting starts there when the rate is above about 0.42, and growing swings (divergence) start above about 0.83.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with standard deviation 1.5 × √((64 − B)/(63 B)) per setting, so it is 1.5 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2 × their true step length. The orange arrow is the step the ball takes next. The grey arrow is −learning rate × the true slope. The readout gives the true length.",
    "Each run lasts 40 updates, one every 3 host steps (10 a second at 1×). The run is marked diverged when the loss goes above 4.8, the loss at the bowl's steep edge.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed, the run number and the step number. Reset replays run 0. A new run uses fresh noise and keeps the last path as the ghost.",
    "Each trail holds 41 points: the start plus one per step. A ball outside the drawn square is shown at its edge. The loss readout keeps the true value."
  ]
}